a spring storm or a turning of the tide  whatever the ultimate toll covert 19 has  sparked a crisis that has engulfed us  all our health our finances our everyday  lives it's a moment that says the  Chancellor has brought the country  together in shared sacrifice but has it  in one sense we really are all in it  together never before has there been a  recession as affected quite so many  people across the country from all walks  of life but in other senses recessions  are I'm afraid inherently unequal some  people keep their jobs other people lose  them some people thrive other people  suffer that's just the nature of it  especially when you're facing an  economic storm the likes of which we've  never seen before and what we're  learning is that the impact of the virus  both on health and on the economy varies  enormous ly it's not even quite how  you'd assume yes cities have been more  affected in both respects but the death  rate here in Portsmouth has been below  the national average and there's less  evidence of a lock-down effect on  movement and social distancing here in  Lambeth in South London the death rate  is more than three times as high than in  Portsmouth one of the most badly  affected parts of the country so key  workers here like bus driver Pat a  particularly vulnerable we are not only  together we are we are the cleaners we  are the care workers where the nurses  where the bus driver we're doing all the  jobs that you know with it with the  general public we are I'm close with  them so we are gonna get it and it is  that concern that particular social and  ethnic groups are especially exposed  which is now becoming clear according to  figures from the Office of National  Statistics the impact of the virus is  being differently felt around the  country so the area with the least  deprivation has seen 25 deaths per  100,000 of the population the area with  the most deprivation has seen 55 deaths  per 100,000 it is about a 118 percent  difference between different areas and  it's not just about deprivation  different ethnic groups are facing  different outcomes as well according to  research from the Institute for Fiscal  Studies black Caribbean people are more  likely to die than many other ethnic  groups of this disease and also more  likely to be in key work in particular  they point out that a third of black  African workers  our key workers compared with only 10%  of white British workers the government  now says it's worried and looking into  these statistics of course this is only  part of the story  what about key workers who actually  can't work le from Surrey as a cleaner  or at least she was before the virus a  mother of three she's had to cancel all  her jobs simply to protect her clients  she and her husband are now trying to  get by on his income as a gardener and  working tax credits have worked at all  at the moment and just for the safety of  my clients I can't risk it we've had a  lot of sleepless nights panicking that  we won't be able to feed the kids and  like oh my god what's gonna happen  that's we're struggling through we've  got their back in Portsmouth Ashleigh a  supermarket worker counts herself as one  of the lucky ones she lives at home with  her parents and is now working nights  stacking shelves and picking food for  online orders to reduce the risk of  getting the disease there are measures  in place to protect us but of course it  is a little bit worrying like when maybe  customers aren't so that they don't  follow it as well as maybe they should  but you just kind of got to do the best  you can under circumstances as these  testimonies and the statistics that  underpin them mount up it's becoming  clearer about who is bearing most of the  risk of the disease and the economic  burden we are all in it together but  some more than others  Eddie Conway Sky News
 

